d Science\nMechanical and Mechatronics Engineering\nLearn to innovate through motion and design the future.\nMechanical engineers are movers. They move heat, fluids, machine parts and people. They\u2019re uniquely qualified to analyze and mathematically model anything that moves, whether it be the flow of air over a wing or through a jet engine, an automobile suspension, wind turbines or even the human body.\nMechatronics engineers add the spark. They make mechanical systems work by combining them with electronics, electrical components and computers. If a mechanical system needs things like machine vision, data logging, artificial intelligence or computer navigation, mechatronics engineers make that happen.\nAs a mechanical and mechatronics engineering student at Memorial, you\u2019ll work with internationally-recognized professors, gain hands-on experience designing projects in state of the art labs, and graduate with two full years of industry experience through our mandatory co-op program.\nAfter that, it\u2019s time for your dream career. Mechanical and mechatronics engineers are in high demand across a huge range of sectors, including automotive, biomedical, renewable energy, aerospace, agriculture, offshore oil and gas, manufacturing, paper, environmental, and power generation.\nClick below to learn more about each discipline:\nNews\nView All\nAPPLY NOW!\nCo-Operative Education\nAccreditation\nNewfoundland and Labrador's University\nFacebook\nInstagram\nYoutube\nLinked\nTiktok\nWe acknowledge that the lands on which Memorial\nUniversity's campuses are situated are in the traditional\nterritories of diverse Indigenous groups, and\nwe acknowledge with respect the diverse histories\nand cultures of the Beothuk, Mi'kmaq, Innu, and\nInuit of this province.\nA-Z Directory\nAccessibility\nCareers\nContact\nEmergency\nPrivacy\nWeb Feedback\nSite Map\n| Last Updated: Jul 14, 2026\nCopyright @2026 Memorial University of Newfoundland.\nNewfoundland and Labrador, Canada.